import{_ as o,o as i,c as t,a3 as s}from"./chunks/framework.BwzYn71E.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"4.0 Building the Science of Blocking-Based Production on Blocking-Flow Theory (Slow Updates)","description":"","frontmatter":{"translationSource":{"title":"4.0 从阻塞流理论基石上建起阻塞生产学的大厦（缓更）","archivedAt":"2026-10-27","url":"https://docs.qq.com/aio/DTmluZ1diWlpQZldw?p=SI1crfpBOI1mEcT4TsqTBX","scope":"page"},"translationLang":"en","head":[["link",{"rel":"canonical","href":"https://kokobird28144.github.io/endfield-skydoc/4-0-building-the-science-of-blocking-based-production-on-blocking-flow"}],["link",{"rel":"alternate","hreflang":"en","href":"https://kokobird28144.github.io/endfield-skydoc/4-0-building-the-science-of-blocking-based-production-on-blocking-flow"}],["link",{"rel":"alternate","hreflang":"zh-CN","href":"https://kokobird28144.github.io/endfield-skydoc/zh/4-0-building-the-science-of-blocking-based-production-on-blocking-flow"}],["link",{"rel":"alternate","hreflang":"x-default","href":"https://kokobird28144.github.io/endfield-skydoc/4-0-building-the-science-of-blocking-based-production-on-blocking-flow"}]]},"headers":[],"relativePath":"4-0-building-the-science-of-blocking-based-production-on-blocking-flow.md","filePath":"logistics/src/page-SI1crfpBOI1mEcT4TsqTBX.md"}'),n={name:"4-0-building-the-science-of-blocking-based-production-on-blocking-flow.md"};function a(r,e,l,c,d,p){return i(),t("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[s('<div class="logistics-content"><span class="logistics-source-anchor" id="_4-0-从阻塞流理论基石上建起阻塞生产学的大厦-缓更" aria-hidden="true"></span><h1 id="_4-0-building-the-science-of-blocking-based-production-on-blocking-flow-theory-slow-updates" tabindex="-1">4.0 Building the Science of Blocking-Based Production on Blocking-Flow Theory (Slow Updates) <a class="header-anchor" href="#_4-0-building-the-science-of-blocking-based-production-on-blocking-flow-theory-slow-updates" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;4\\.0 Building the Science of Blocking-Based Production on Blocking-Flow Theory (Slow Updates)&quot;">​</a></h1><blockquote><p>Source page ID: <code>SI1crfpBOI1mEcT4TsqTBX</code> ｜ <a href="https://docs.qq.com/aio/DTmluZ1diWlpQZldw?p=SI1crfpBOI1mEcT4TsqTBX" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Open in Tencent Docs</a></p></blockquote><p><span id="source-block-JslOqvCriLjbhKfC601gZ6"></span></p><span class="logistics-source-anchor" id="_1-阻塞流理论简要回顾" aria-hidden="true"></span><h3 id="_1-brief-review-of-blocking-flow-theory" tabindex="-1">1 Brief Review of Blocking-Flow Theory <a class="header-anchor" href="#_1-brief-review-of-blocking-flow-theory" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;1 Brief Review of Blocking-Flow Theory&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Blocking-flow theory <a href="/endfield-skydoc/flow-theory-for-idealized-logistics-systems">Flow Theory for Idealized Logistics Systems</a> is a cornerstone of Endfield industrial production. Essentially relying only on Splitter/Converger round-robin rules, it describes how blocked or flowing connected paths cause ports to be skipped, correctly describing item allocation and belt behavior. As long as belt delivery remains, this theory will probably remain useful even if priority, the four-item stall, etc. are fixed as bugs.</p><blockquote><p>If one belt is blocked, of course you can only send items to others. What else do you expect? If Hypergryph changes even that, the eagle is truly divine.</p></blockquote><p>Informally, items moving through belts and logistics components are in a flowing state; stationary items are in a blocked state. A simple intuitive conclusion is that without cycles in the flow paths (a directed graph)—no item returns to merge further upstream—routes are basically all flowing, with at most brief blocking from phase conflicts or insufficient path buffering.</p><p>The simple oscillating-power arrangement and more complex splitting structure below are both of this kind.</p><p><img src="/endfield-skydoc/logistics/src/assets/images/img-efdd5b7a250f5cb3.q90-a7733d5e92049968.webp" alt="40 Building the Science of Blocking-Based Production on Blocking-Flow Theory (Slow Updates)"></p><p><img src="/endfield-skydoc/logistics/src/assets/images/img-f6be4a0c0f3447cc.q90-55926f2ad4f84d94.webp" alt="40 Building the Science of Blocking-Based Production on Blocking-Flow Theory (Slow Updates)"></p><p>A simple blocking example is the halving limiter below left. Some output returns to the initial Converger; accepting it all would make the short middle section carry 1.5 times belt capacity, which is impossible. Some blocking must occur. In fact, Depot Unloader output falls to 15, the middle branch remains at full rate 30, half splits off, and final output is 15.</p><p>Recirculation need not cause blocking. The 2x5 divider below right returns one of six branches, but Converger flow is input 15 + recirculation 3 = 18, below belt capacity.</p><p><img src="/endfield-skydoc/logistics/src/assets/images/img-6ce6be1cbe0a457d.q90-656ecedd7db4fd19.webp" alt="40 Building the Science of Blocking-Based Production on Blocking-Flow Theory (Slow Updates)"></p><p><img src="/endfield-skydoc/logistics/src/assets/images/img-9bfb2e03fb817a02.q90-ba0299229594a852.webp" alt="40 Building the Science of Blocking-Based Production on Blocking-Flow Theory (Slow Updates)"></p><p>One important problem is <strong>solving flow rates in a blocking graph</strong>: given Splitters, Convergers, paths, maximum input, and maximum output, calculate actual flow on each internal path. Below is a 2/5 limiter&#39;s construction graph and solution. It omits secondary details such as belt lengths and shapes, emphasizing connectivity. Red paths are blocked; gray paths are not.</p><p>The lesson is: <span class="mark-purple"><strong>designed allocation ≠ actual allocation</strong></span>. Splitter 1 outputs 2/5, 2/5, 1/5; the lower-left path, intended for 1/3, actually carries 1/5. Converger 5 receives excessive input and blocks this path, reducing its flow; the reduction is shared equally between the other two routes, increasing them. <strong>Blocked edges affect flow, redistributing it.</strong></p><p><img src="/endfield-skydoc/logistics/src/assets/images/img-c6b32f977a87f3ff.q90-eef68420d6a66aed.webp" alt="40 Building the Science of Blocking-Based Production on Blocking-Flow Theory (Slow Updates)"></p><p><span id="source-block-P2pJymPd7a3tEz7m7TvTVx"></span></p><span class="logistics-source-anchor" id="_2-与阻塞生产学的联系" aria-hidden="true"></span><h3 id="_2-connection-to-blocking-based-production" tabindex="-1">2 Connection to Blocking-Based Production <a class="header-anchor" href="#_2-connection-to-blocking-based-production" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;2 Connection to Blocking-Based Production&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Blocking-flow theory mainly considers blocking caused by splitting/merging structures. Blocking-based production adds a major difference: <strong>machines</strong>. Most machines faithfully do one thing: <u>when inputs meet requirements, start production and convert inputs into products</u>.</p><p>Below, a Fitting Unit processes Amethyst Fiber into parts 1:1, matching belt throughput. Its only effect is 2 seconds&#39; processing delay (a belt actually takes 6 seconds to travel three tiles). Downstream, its products behave exactly like Amethyst Fiber without the machine. <strong>Structures containing such simple machines can use blocking-flow theory unchanged for blocking analysis alone.</strong></p><blockquote><p>It would be incredible if a future processed item traveled more slowly on belts.</p></blockquote><p><img src="/endfield-skydoc/logistics/src/assets/images/img-2f0791772d2399e3.q90-1c56764d68be2c77.webp" alt="40 Building the Science of Blocking-Based Production on Blocking-Flow Theory (Slow Updates)"></p><p>Slightly more complex machines, such as Moulding Units, <strong>turn incoming inputs into products at 1/2</strong>. From a blocking-flow perspective, half the path flow disappears. Current blocking-flow theory has no treatment for such intermediate flow removal; this is a new topic for production research. Downstream logistics can still be analyzed with blocking-flow theory, even though output becomes 1/2 its original rate and some formerly blocked edges stop blocking. We must therefore investigate different maximum input rates.</p><p>Below, 30 Amethyst Fiber become 15 Amethyst Bottles, then pass through a halving limiter. All 15 bottles pass; direct input of 30 Fiber would pass only 15. Blocking-flow theory clearly establishes that this structure limits inputs above 15 to 15, otherwise retaining the input rate.</p><p><img src="/endfield-skydoc/logistics/src/assets/images/img-8b6dfaeb3860f34a.q90-9a85dde0cfd5fc95.webp" alt="40 Building the Science of Blocking-Based Production on Blocking-Flow Theory (Slow Updates)"></p><p>More complex cases involve <strong>multi-input recipes</strong>. If one input is insufficient, another blocks. Below, insufficient Origocrust makes Amethyst Fiber block inside the machine and overflow through the Splitter downstream. Without the machine, Fiber could split equally downstream. The machine creates new blocking, but the resulting allocation <strong>still follows rules similar to blocking-flow theory</strong>.</p><p><img src="/endfield-skydoc/logistics/src/assets/images/img-9e9aac82ae9e08b4.q90-8e01e87c0b61ee33.webp" alt="40 Building the Science of Blocking-Based Production on Blocking-Flow Theory (Slow Updates)"></p><p>Other machines have unique properties: crucibles prioritize output of external inputs, creating pass-through, while transmuters freeload on Xiranite. Generally, summarize each machine&#39;s logistics rules and determine whether blocking-flow analysis applies.</p><p>Also, since processed products generally do not return for further processing, cycles are rare in blocking-based production, simplifying some analysis.</p><p><span id="source-block-I9OJ1HhtnupLXauaswdbHp"></span></p><span class="logistics-source-anchor" id="_3-所关心的共通问题" aria-hidden="true"></span><h3 id="_3-shared-questions-of-interest" tabindex="-1">3 Shared Questions of Interest <a class="header-anchor" href="#_3-shared-questions-of-interest" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;3 Shared Questions of Interest&quot;">​</a></h3><p>We often study blocking in machine buffers or the depot caused by constraints. Logistics structures then <strong>redistribute flow</strong>, <strong>changing production</strong>. Hence a central problem is likewise <span class="mark-purple"><strong>solving flow rates in a blocking-production graph</strong></span>, now considering machine rates and bottleneck input supplies as well as splitting/merging.</p><p>Borrow Section 2.5&#39;s diagram directly. Of 60 Cuprium, Splitters send 3/4 to canisters and 1/4 to bottles. Downstream Separator Core blocking allows consumption of only 22 canisters/min, blocking Cuprium in the Moulding Unit; <strong>excess flow diverts to the Moulding Unit</strong>. Using Section 2.5 notation, <strong>designed Cuprium flow to the canister Moulding Unit is 45-, and to the Cuprium Canister Moulding Unit 45+</strong>. After balancing-type blocking, actual rates are 44 and 46 respectively, redistributing flow.</p><p><img src="/endfield-skydoc/logistics/src/assets/images/img-bf8d97c0ee1e8e60.q90-22256e10204790e8.webp" alt="40 Building the Science of Blocking-Based Production on Blocking-Flow Theory (Slow Updates)"></p><p>Besides calculating balanced flow and production, we must ask whether balance can be reached. Blocking-graph solutions need not be unique: different initial states may evolve to different results, already observed in-game.</p><p><span class="mark-grey"><s>(Add an example or reference</s></span><span class="mark-grey"><s>△</s></span><span class="mark-grey">~~ here.) (Where would I get an example? For given graph input/output flows, there is only one result, lol.)~~</span></p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><img src="/endfield-skydoc/logistics/src/assets/images/img-2ae30eaf66d9912f.gif" alt="Image"></td><td><img src="/endfield-skydoc/logistics/src/assets/images/img-614a951899056e7c.gif" alt="Image"></td></tr></tbody></table><p>Observe the left Splitter: behavior differs under different blocking states because some Originium already exists in the right-hand system. <strong>The two systems have identical splitting/merging designs</strong>, yet neither state naturally evolves into the other.</p><p>Given similar underlying logic, does blocking-based production have similar problems? Two important questions are: <span class="mark-purple"><strong>for a given production plan, are there several feasible flow/production allocations, or a unique one?</strong></span></p><p>Or, <span class="mark-purple"><strong>starting with empty storage, can we evolve to the desired production result?</strong></span> This directly affects whether a newly placed blueprint automatically reaches the required stable production.</p><p><a href="/endfield-skydoc/3-1-the-beginnings-of-automatic-balancing-xiranite-and-originium-powders">3.1 The Beginnings of Automatic Balancing: Xiranite and Originium Powder’s Love–Hate Relationship</a> already shows convergence to different outcomes with incorrect priority. With correct priority, no multiple solutions or failures to stabilize have yet been found, but proof is still needed. See the paper for related discussion.</p><blockquote><p>Translation note: The final canister/bottle example contains apparent source errors: it names canisters twice and gives 45+ and 46 where Section 2.5’s corresponding bottle stream is 15+ and 16. The values and labels retain the source.</p></blockquote></div>',1)])])}const h=o(n,[["render",a]]);export{u as __pageData,h as default};
